analysis:
Examined ProviderLoop.swift head lines 1-1192. The only change is lines 484-497, which add add-on hold state: AddonHoldBook, a drain-and-unload task, a lease monitor, an AddonIPCServer local socket, and test seams (addonHoldPathsOverride, addonPeerAuthenticatorOverride at line 495, a wall clock and a drain timeout). DaemonState+Coding.swift adds an optional addonHold key that is encoded and decoded through decodeIfPresent and encodeIfPresent (head lines 18, 56, 93).

This introduces new local attack surface. A local IPC socket lets another process ask the provider to drain and unload models. It falls under TB-003 (operator vs process) and adjacent availability concerns (T-016/T-029-like DoS). It also resembles the TB-010 lease pattern, where T-044 covers local impersonation.

The implementation is in ProviderLoop+AddonHold.swift and AddonIPCServer, which were not supplied. I could not verify:
- peer authentication: what the default Authenticate checks, such as uid or code signature;
- socket path permissions;
- lease bounds;
- whether holds can block attestation challenge handling or coordinator registration;
- whether a hold interacts safely with the shutdown, update and reslice gates.

The peer-authenticator override is a test seam on actor state. It must not be reachable from production configuration.

The daemon-state addonHold snapshot is written to the state file. Its contents (AddonHoldSnapshot) are unseen; check that it holds no request IDs, prompt-derived data or tokens (T-058-like leakage).

No concrete regression is visible in the supplied lines; they are declarations and coding plumbing only. The threat model has no entry for this add-on IPC boundary, so it likely needs a new trust boundary entry once the implementation is reviewed.
